WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:02.040
YUFENG GUO: A team
at Google set out

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.830
to make a game of
Pictionary more interesting

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:07.020
and ended up with the
world's largest doodling

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:10.860
dataset and a powerful machine
learning model to boot.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.090
How did they do it?

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:15.040
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:16.650
Welcome to "AI
Adventures," where

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:21.310
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:22.500
My name is Yufeng Guo.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.540
And on this episode,
we're going to check out

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.390
the largest dataset
of hand-drawn doodles

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:29.250
and see what we can do with it.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:31.590
"Quick, Draw!" was a
game that was initially

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:35.370
featured at Google I/O
in 2016 as a game where

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:36.990
one player would
be prompted to draw

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:39.480
a picture of an object
and the other player

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:42.570
would need to guess what it was,
just your standard Pictionary

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:43.650
game.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:47.680
In 2017, the team took
that idea a step further

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.270
and used the Sketch-RNN
model from the Magenta team

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:53.820
at Google Research
to try to predict

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.940
what the player was
drawing in real time

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.280
instead requiring
a second player.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.680
The game is now available
online and has collected

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.024
millions of hand-drawn doodles.

00:01:04.024 --> 00:01:05.940
Let's take a look at
some of the drawings that

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.280
have come out of "Quick, Draw!"

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.650
Here we see some
broccoli being drawn

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.480
by many different players.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.330
Oceans are depicted in
slightly different ways

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.530
by different players
all over the world.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:20.730
It's just a lot of fun
to browse the dataset.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:23.610
And if you find something
that seems out of place,

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:26.730
you can fix it right
there on the page

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:29.590
and make the data
better for everyone.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:31.530
In fact, the team has
open-sourced this data

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.760
and in a variety of formats.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.790
You can learn more
at their GitHub page.

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:37.350
There are four formats.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:42.270
First up is the raw files
themselves, stored in .ndjson.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:46.980
These files encode the full set
of information for each doodle.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.890
It contains timing
information for every stroke

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:51.600
of every picture drawn.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.880
And there's also a
simplified version

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.780
stored in the same format, which
has some pre-processing applied

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.090
to help normalize the data.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:04.440
This simplified version is also
available as a binary format

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.500
for more efficient
storage and transfer.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.060
And it comes with
examples of how

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:13.950
to read out these binary files
using both Python and Node.js.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:15.720
The fourth format
is interesting.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:21.130
It takes in the simplified
data and renders it as 28-by-28

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:26.310
grayscale bitmap in
NumPy .npy format.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:29.230
You can load it using np.load().

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:31.740
Why 28-by-28 pixels?

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:35.640
Well, it's a perfect replacement
for any existing code you might

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:38.670
have for processing MNIST data.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:41.100
So if you're looking
for something fancier

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:44.460
than 10 hand-written digits,
you can try processing

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:48.810
over 300 different classes
of hand-drawn doodles.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:51.420
So ready to take on
that dataset and train

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:53.379
your own recurrent neural net?

00:02:53.379 --> 00:02:54.795
Check out the
tutorial I've linked

00:02:54.795 --> 00:02:57.040
in the description down below.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.430
And if you're want to
explore the data some more,

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:03.280
you can visualize the "Quick,
Draw!" dataset using Facets.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.520
The Facets team has
taken the liberty

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:09.240
of hosting the data online
and even given us some presets

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:10.500
to play around with.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.840
We can load up
some random chairs

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:14.970
and see how different
players drew the chairs

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:16.570
from all over the world.

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:18.240
We can also see
which drawings were

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.859
recognized as chairs, which
ones didn't quite make the cut.

00:03:21.859 --> 00:03:23.400
There's a number of
preset views that

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.350
are also worth
playing around with

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:28.650
and serve as great starting
points for further exploration

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:30.210
and analysis.

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:34.200
I have one final idea for what
you can do with this dataset.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.530
I noticed that while there
are hexagons and pandas,

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:40.560
there aren't any pentagons
or koalas in the dataset.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.740
You can create a new class
to extend this dataset

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:45.750
and train your
model to recognize,

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:48.570
not only in the many different
classes already present,

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:51.150
but also in your own
custom image class.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:54.540
Whether it's your company logo,
school emblem, or something

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:58.380
else entirely, make
the dataset your own.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:01.260
Thanks for watching this episode
of "Cloud AI Adventures."

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:03.000
And if you enjoyed
it, please like it

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:06.510
and consider subscribing to get
all the latest episodes right

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:07.830
when they come out.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:10.300
For now, check out the
"Quick, Draw!" dataset

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:13.362
and see what insights
you can glean from it.

